---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import Walkthrough from '../components/Walkthrough.astro';
---

<Site title="Connect your AI to the apps you use">
  <main id="main" class="home-content">
    <header class="home-intro">
      <p class="eyebrow">Open source · Runs on your computer</p>
      <h1>Connect your AI to the apps you use.</h1>
      <p class="intro-description">
        Blindkit lets your AI read data and take actions in your apps. You decide what it can do.
        Your API keys stay out of the AI’s code.
      </p>
      <div class="intro-actions">
        <a class="button" href="/docs/quickstart/">
          Set up Blindkit <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/">
          Read the introduction
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="small-note">Free to use. No Blindkit account or database needed.</p>
    </header>

    <section id="how-it-works" class="home-section example-section">
      <div class="section-label">
        <h2>One connection. You choose the actions.</h2>
        <span class="small-note">An interactive example</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        Connect an app, ask your AI for help, and let Blindkit check the request before it goes
        through.
      </p>
      <Walkthrough />
    </section>

    <section id="your-rules" class="home-section">
      <h2>A little help, with clear limits.</h2>
      <p>Give your AI useful tools without handing over every decision.</p>
      <dl class="feature-list">
        <div>
          <dt>Choose when to ask.</dt>
          <dd>
            Allow an action, ask for approval, or block it. Changes ask first by default. An
            approval applies to one specific request.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>Keep keys out of the conversation.</dt>
          <dd>
            Blindkit adds your API key when it sends a request. Your AI does not need to read or
            handle the key.
          </dd>
        </div>
        <div>
          <dt>See what happened.</dt>
          <dd>
            A local activity log records each request and its result. If Blindkit cannot record a
            request, it does not send it.
          </dd>
        </div>
      </dl>
      <a class="text-link" href="/docs/security/">
        How protection works, and its limits <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
    </section>

    <section id="setup" class="home-section">
      <h2>Start with the included example.</h2>
      <p>
        Download the code and connect a sample app. Then add Blindkit to an AI app that supports
        MCP, the standard for connecting AI to tools.
      </p>
      <div class="install-example">
        <div class="code-label">
          In your terminal <span>Node 22 or 24 · pnpm 10</span>
        </div>
        <pre><code>git clone https://github.com/aliarain/blindkit.git
cd blindkit
pnpm install --frozen-lockfile
pnpm build
node dist/cli.js init
node dist/cli.js add examples/posts.openapi.json --name posts</code></pre>
      </div>
      <a class="text-link" href="/docs/quickstart/">
        Continue with the setup guide <span aria-hidden="true">→</span>
      </a>
      <div class="release-note">
        <span class="release-tag">v0.1 alpha</span>
        <p>
          This is an early release for trying things out. It has not had an independent security
          review. Start with the example before connecting important data.
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section id="questions" class="home-section questions-section">
      <h2>A few things to know.</h2>
      <details>
        <summary>Which apps can I connect?</summary>
        <p>
          Apps with an OpenAPI 3.0 JSON file describing their API. Some formats and sign-in methods
          are not supported yet. Check the <a href="/docs/config/">supported formats</a> before
          connecting an app.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Where does Blindkit run?</summary>
        <p>
          On your computer. Your settings and activity log are local files. Requests go to the APIs
          you connect. You do not need a hosted Blindkit account or a database.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Can my AI approve its own changes?</summary>
        <p>
          No. When approval is required, your AI app must show you an approval form. If it cannot,
          Blindkit stops the request. Use an AI app you trust to show the form and report your
          answer.
        </p>
      </details>
      <details>
        <summary>Is it open source?</summary>
        <p>
          Yes. The code is available on <a href="https://github.com/aliarain/blindkit">GitHub</a>{' '}
          under the MIT license. You can read it, run it, and change it.
        </p>
      </details>
    </section>
  </main>
</Site>
